<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>CQ - Evaluating the Convergence and Value of the Improper Integral (Infinite interval of integration)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="border: 2px solid #000; background-color: #f0f0f0; padding: 10px; color: green; min-width:100px; display: inline-block">
<h2>Improper Integrals Involving Unbounded Intervals</h2> 
<p>This involves integration over an interval whose upper limit, (or lower limit) grows without bound. You can think of this as evaluating the area of an unbounded region.</p>
<p>An example of such integrals is \[\int_{-\infty}^{3}e^{2x}dx=\lim_{t\to-\infty}\int_{t}^{3}e^{2x}dx\] or \[\int_{1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{2x^{3}}dx=\lim_{t\to\infty}\int_{1}^{t}\frac{1}{2x^{3}}dx\]</p>
</div>
<p> Consider the following integral \(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx\). This is improper integral because  [[input:ans1a]] [[validation:ans1a]][[feedback:prt1a]]</p>
<div style="border: 2px solid #000; background-color: #f0f0f0; padding: 10px; color: green; min-width:100px; display: inline-block">
<p> To evaluate this type of an improper integral, we first express it as a limit. That is  \(\displaystyle\lim_{t\to\infty} \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{t} {@fx@}\,dx\). We then use the <a href="https://activecalculus.org/single/sec-4-4-FTC.html" target="_blank">Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</a>
 to evaluate the integral \(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{t} {@fx@}\,dx\).</p>
 </div>
<p>To simplify the integral, we will need to make a \(u\) substitution. Here \(u=\) [[input:ans1b]] and \(du =\)[[input:ans1c]]\(dx\) [[validation:ans1b]][[validation:ans1c]].</p>
<p> Making this change of variable, the new integrand is given by [[input:ans1d]][[validation:ans1d]] and the limits of integration are
<ul>
  <li>lower limit =[[input:ans1e]][[validation:ans1e]] </li>
  <li>upper limit = [[input:ans1f]][[validation:ans1f]]</li>
</ul>

 So that the antiderivative is \(\displaystyle \int{@func_2@}\,du=\) [[input:ans1g]].[[validation:ans1g]]</p>
<p> Evaluating the antiderivative at the limits of integration gives </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{t}{@fx@}\,dx=\)[[input:ans1h]] [[validation:ans1h]][[feedback:prt1b]]</p> 

<div style="border: 2px solid #000; background-color: #f0f0f0; padding: 10px; color: green; min-width:100px; display: inline-block">
<h2>Convergence and divergence of an improper integral</h2>
<p>The limit \(\displaystyle \lim_{t\to\infty} \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{t}{@fx@}\,dx\)
 is either finite or infinite (or it doesn’t exist). With these possibilities in mind, we introduce the following terminology.</p>

If \(f(x)\) is nonnegative for \(x\geq a\), then we say that the improper integral \(\displaystyle \int_{a}^{\infty}f(x)dx\) converges provided that \[\lim_{t\to\infty} \int_{a}^{t}f(x)dx\] exists and is finite. otherwise, we say that \(\displaystyle \int_{a}^{\infty}f(x)dx\) diverges.
</div>
<div style="border: 2px solid #000; background-color: #f0f0f0; padding: 10px; color: green; min-width:100px; display: inline-block">
<p> We now want to determine if the integral \(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx\) converges or diverges.  To do this, we evaluate the limit as \(t\longrightarrow \infty\) of your answer above. <em>You might want to review examples <a href="https://math.libretexts.org/@go/page/10189" target="_blank"> 2.5.5 and 2.5.6 </a> to refresh your memory on how to evaluate limits of this nature.</em>

(Note: \( \infty\) is entered as <b>inf</b> and \( -\infty\) is entered as <b>minf</b>). 
</p>
</div>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx=\lim_{t\to\infty} \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{t}{@fx@}\,dx=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>Therefore, the integral is [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3> 
<p> First this is improper integral because the limits of integration include infinity.</p>
<p> So we express the integral as a limit. That is  \(\displaystyle\lim_{t\to\infty} \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{t} {@fx@}\,dx\). </p>
<p>To simplify the integral, we will need to make a \(u\) substitution. Here \(u={@ta_1b@}\) and \(du ={@ta_1c@} dx\) .</p>
<p> Making this change of variable, the integrand is given by  \({@ta_1d@}\) and the limits of integration are
<ul>
  <li>lower limit  \(={@ta_1e@}\)</li>
  <li>upper limit  \(u={@ta_1f@}\)</li>
</ul>
 So that the antiderivative is  \({@ta_1g@}\) </p>
<p> Evaluating the antiderivative at the limits of integration gives </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{t}{@fx@}\,dx={@ta_1h@}\)</p> 
<p> Finally evaluating the limit as \(t\longrightarrow \infty\) of your answer above gives the value of the integral. (Note: \( \infty\) is entered as <b>inf</b> and \( -\infty\) is entered as <b>minf</b>). </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx=\lim_{t\to\infty} \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{t}{@fx@}\,dx={@ta_1@}\) </>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
